            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must determine all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. their slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal covering)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with  \n\n\\[\n2\\le c\\le n+1\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{\\text{all}}=\\{D_c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince the index set \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-2+1=n\n\\]\n\nelements, \\(|\\mathcal D_{\\text{all}}|=n\\).  All these lines have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore **not** sunny.  Consequently the value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Isolated sunny line through any point)\n\nLet \\(P=(p_x,p_y)\\in T_n\\).  \nThere exists a line \\(L\\) through \\(P\\) whose slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\]\n\nand such that \\(L\\cap T_n=\\{P\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider only non‑vertical lines; a vertical line has slope \\(\\infty\\) and is prohibited.  \nFor any finite slope \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) the line through \\(P\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL_\\lambda:\\; y-p_y=\\lambda(x-p_x). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L_\\lambda\\) also passes through another point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\), then from (1)\n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{b-p_y}{\\,a-p_x\\,}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus each distinct extra point \\((a,b)\\) of \\(T_n\\) determines a single rational value of \\(\\lambda\\).  \nSince \\(T_n\\) is finite, the set  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\in T_n,\\;(a,b)\\neq P\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis finite.  \n\nAdd the two slopes that are explicitly forbidden for a sunny line:\n\n\\[\nS' = S\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S'\\) is still a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb Q\\).  \nBecause the rational numbers are infinite, we can choose a rational number  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus S'.\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(\\lambda\\) is automatically different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); it is finite, so the corresponding line \\(L_\\lambda\\) is not vertical (hence its slope is not \\(\\infty\\)).  \n\nBy construction \\(\\lambda\\notin S\\), therefore (2) cannot hold for any \\((a,b)\\neq P\\) in \\(T_n\\). Consequently the line \\(L_\\lambda\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only at \\(P\\).  \n\nFinally, the slope \\(\\lambda\\) avoids the three prohibited values, so \\(L_\\lambda\\) is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n1. **Selection of diagonal sums to be replaced.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n:=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   has exactly \\(n\\) elements. Choose any subset  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\subseteq S_n\n   \\]\n\n   with \\(|C|=k\\) (possible