ap T_n=\\{P\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider only non‑vertical lines; a vertical line has slope \\(\\infty\\) and is prohibited.  \nFor any finite slope \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) the line through \\(P\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL_\\lambda:\\; y-p_y=\\lambda(x-p_x). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L_\\lambda\\) also passes through another point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\), then from (1)\n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{b-p_y}{\\,a-p_x\\,}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus each distinct extra point \\((a,b)\\) of \\(T_n\\) determines a single rational value of \\(\\lambda\\).  \nSince \\(T_n\\) is finite, the set  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}\\;\\Big|\\;(a,b)\\in T_n,\\;(a,b)\\neq P\\Bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nis finite.  \n\nAdd the two slopes that are explicitly forbidden for a sunny line:\n\n\\[\nS' = S\\cup\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S'\\) is still a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb Q\\).  \nBecause the rational numbers are infinite, we can choose a rational number  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus S'.\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(\\lambda\\) is automatically different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); it is finite, so the corresponding line \\(L_\\lambda\\) is not vertical (hence its slope is not \\(\\infty\\)).  \n\nBy construction \\(\\lambda\\notin S\\), therefore (2) cannot hold for any \\((a,b)\\neq P\\) in \\(T_n\\). Consequently the line \\(L_\\lambda\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only at \\(P\\).  \n\nFinally, the slope \\(\\lambda\\) avoids the three prohibited values, so \\(L_\\lambda\\) is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n1. **Selection of diagonal sums to be replaced.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n:=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   has exactly \\(n\\) elements. Choose any subset  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\subseteq S_n\n   \\]\n\n   with \\(|C|=k\\) (possible because \\(k\\le n\\)).  \n\n2. **Choosing a point on each selected diagonal.**  \n   For each \\(c_i\\) pick a point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(a_i,b_i)\\in T_n\\quad\\text{with}\\quad a_i+b_i=c_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Such a point exists; for instance \\((1,c_i-1)\\) satisfies the required inequalities because \\(c_i\\ge2\\) and \\(c_i\\le n+1\\).  \n   The points \\(P_i\\) are distinct, since their sums \\(c_i\\) are distinct.\n\n3. **Sunny lines through the chosen points.**  \n   Apply Lemma 2 to each \\(P_i\\) to obtain a sunny line \\(L_i\\) that contains \\(P_i\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct, because each contains a different point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Remaining diagonal (non‑sunny) lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal D = \\{\\,D_c \\mid c\\in S_n\\setminus C\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(|S_n|=n\\) and \\(|C|=k\\), we have \\(|\\mathcal D| = n-k\\).  \n   Every line in \\(\\mathcal D\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.\n\n5. **The final family of lines.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L = \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal D .\n   \\]\n\n   Its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L| = k + (n-k) = n .\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny.\n\n6. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and put \\(c=a+b\\).  \n\n   *If \\(c\\notin C\\)*, then \\(D_c\\in\\mathcal D\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on that diagonal line.  \n\n   *If \\(c=c_i\\in C\\)*, then by construction \\((a,b)=P_i\\); this point lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Explicit example for \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\)\n\nHere \\(S_3=\\{2,3,4\\}\\). Choose \\(C=\\{2,3\\}\\).\n\n* Points on the selected diagonals:  \n  \\(P_2=(1,1)\\) (since \\(1+1=2\\)), \\(P_3=(2,1)\\) (since \\(2+1=3\\)).\n\n* Sunny lines obtained from Lemma 2 (any slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and not hitting another point of \\(T_3\\); for instance):  \n\n  \\[\n  L_2:\\; y-1 = 2(x-1) \\qquad\\text{(slope }2),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  L_3:\\; y-1 = 3(x-2) \\qquad\\text{(slope }3).\n  \\]\n\n* Remaining diagonal line: \\(D_4:\\; x+y=4\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_3\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n\\((1,1)\\in L_2\\), \\((2,1)\\in L_3\\), \\((1,2)\\in D_4\\).  \nThus all required points are covered and exactly two of the three lines are sunny, confirming the construction for \\((3,2)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Optimality\n\nA collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, because each line is either sunny or not. Hence no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realized.\n\nConversely, the construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
