ach of them is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of points that must be covered  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 (All\u2011diagonal covering)\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine the line  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for some \\(c\\) in the above set, hence \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{\\text{all}}=\\{D_c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\\(|\\mathcal D_{\\text{all}}|=n\\) and every line in it has slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \nHence the value \\(k=0\\) is always achievable.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 (Isolated sunny line through any point)\n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(P=(p_x,p_y)\\in T_n\\).  \nThere exists a line \\(L\\) passing through \\(P\\) such that  \n\n* its slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfies \\(\\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) (i.e. the line is sunny), and  \n* \\(L\\cap T_n=\\{P\\}\\) (no other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(L\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider only non\u2011vertical lines; a vertical line has slope \\(\\infty\\) and is therefore forbidden.  \nFor any finite slope \\(\\lambda\\neq -1\\) the line through \\(P\\) is  \n\n\\[\nL_\\lambda:\\; y-p_y=\\lambda\\,(x-p_x). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSuppose that for some \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) we also have \\((a,b)\\in L_\\lambda\\).  \nThen (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus each distinct extra point \\((a,b)\\) of \\(T_n\\) determines a single real number (possibly \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)) that would make the line pass through that point.  \n\nDefine the **finite** set of forbidden slopes  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\Bigl\\{\\frac{b-p_y}{a-p_x}\\;\\