y of lines.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\mathcal L:=\\bigl\\{L_c\\mid c\\in C\\bigr\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal D\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   has  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_c\\) are sunny, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n6. **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(c=a+b\\).  \n\n   *If \\(c\\notin C\\)*, then \\(D_c\\in\\mathcal D\\) and \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\).  \n\n   *If \\(c\\in C\\)*, then by construction \\((a,b)=P_c\\) and \\(P_c\\in L_c\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a configuration satisfying both requirements.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Explicit example for \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\)\n\nHere \\(S_3=\\{2,3,4\\}\\) and we take \\(C=\\{2,3\\}\\).\n\n* Points: \\(P_2=(1,1)\\), \\(P_3=(2,1)\\).\n* Sunny lines (obtained from Lemma\u202f2, any admissible slopes work; we give concrete ones):\n  \\[\n  L_2:\\; y-1 = 2(x-1)\\quad\\text{(slope }2),\n  \\qquad\n  L_3:\\; y-1 = 3(x-2)\\quad\\text{(slope }3).\n  \\]\n* Remaining diagonal: \\(D_4:\\; x+y=4\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) is covered:\n\\((1,1)\\in L_2\\), \\((2,1)\\in L_3\\), \\((1,2)\\in D_4\\).  \nExactly two of the three lines are sunny, confirming the construction.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Optimality\n\nA family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realized.  \nThe construction above works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
