igl(\\mathcal D\\setminus\\{D_2\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_1\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(L_1\\)) is sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\): the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\), while every other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\ge3\\), which is still present.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (adding redundant sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nStarting from the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) of Lemma 2, add the following \\(k-1\\) lines:\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=2x+(i-2)\\qquad (i=2,3,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(2\\), so it is sunny; the intercepts \\((i-2)\\) are distinct, hence the lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from \\(L_1\\).  Moreover each \\(L_i\\) meets the integer lattice at the point \\((1,i-1)\\in T_n\\) (since substituting \\(x=1\\) into (6) gives \\(y=i-1\\)), so the added lines are well defined.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\mathcal L_1\\cup\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_k|= (n-1)+1+(k-1)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the diagonals \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\ge3\\), none of which is sunny.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(c\\ge3\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_c\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).  The points \\((1,i-1)\\) for \\(i=2,\\dots ,k\\) lie on the newly added lines \\(L_i\\); these points are already covered by the diagonals \\(D_{i+1}\\), so no uncovered point is created.  Consequently all points of \\(T_n\\) belong to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\