            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny (they are all parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  Moreover, every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on precisely one line of \\(\\mathcal D\\); indeed, for \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\in D_{a+b}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal D\\) itself is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem, and it contains **no** sunny line.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (configuration with one sunny line)\n\nRemove the diagonal \\(D_2\\) (the line \\(x+y=2\\)), which contains the single point \\((1,1)\\).  \nIntroduce the line  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=2x .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_1\\) is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L_1\\) is sunny.  The family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\bigl(\\mathcal D\\setminus\\{D_2\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_1\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(L_1\\)) is sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\): the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\), while every other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\ge3\\), which is still present.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (adding redundant sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nStarting from the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) of Lemma 2, add the following \\(k-1\\) lines:\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=2x+(i-2)\\qquad (i=2,3,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(2\\), so it is sunny; the intercepts \\((i-2)\\) are distinct, hence the lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_k\\) are pairwise distinct and also distinct from \\(L_1\\).  Moreover each \\(L_i\\) meets the integer lattice at the point \\((1,i-1)\\in T_n\\) (since substituting \\(x=1\\) into (6) gives \\(y=i-1\\)), so the added lines are well defined.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\mathcal L_1\\cup\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_k\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_k|= (n-1)+1+(k-1)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the diagonals \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\ge3\\), none of which is sunny.\n\n**Coverage.**  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(c\\ge3\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_c\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).  The points \\((1,i-1)\\) for \\(i=2,\\dots ,k\\) lie on the newly added lines \\(L_i\\); these points are already covered by the diagonals \\(D_{i+1}\\), so no uncovered point is created.  Consequently all points of \\(T_n\\) belong to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) are attainable.  The complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
